  "constraintReading": "len(nums) can reach 10⁴, so checking every pair means up to about 5·10⁷ sums: O(n²), slow in Python. The O(n) time target rules that out and asks for one pass with O(1) lookups, paid for with O(n) space.",
  "hints": {
    "clarify": "Do you return the numbers or their positions? Can a number pair with itself? Try the list [5, 2, 8] with a target of 10 by hand: 5 + 5 makes 10, but there's only one 5, so it can't count twice. The pair is 2 + 8, at indices 1 and 2.",
    "approach": "Hash map and counting. Every number x has exactly one partner that completes the pair: target − x. Walk through `nums` once and keep a dict from each number you've passed to its index. For each new number, check the dict for its partner. A dict lookup is O(1), so one pass is enough.",
    "whyNot": "Checking every pair works, but it's O(n²) time. Sorting and moving two pointers in from both ends costs O(n log n), and sorting moves the numbers, so you'd have to carry each original index along. A set of seen values can't tell you where the partner was.",
    "slots": {
      "setup": "An empty dict `seen`. The key is a number you've already passed; the value is its index.",
      "loop": "Visit `nums` once with `enumerate`, so you have both the index `i` and the number `x`.",
      "update": "After the lookup misses, store `x` in `seen` with index `i`. Storing it before the lookup could pair `x` with itself.",
      "record": "Work out the partner, target − x, and look it up in `seen`. A hit means you've found the pair: the partner's stored index and `i`.",
      "return": "Return those two indices as soon as the lookup hits. A pair always exists, so the loop never runs out; the empty list after it is only a fallback."
    }
  },
  "solution": {
    "code": "class Solution:\n    def twoSum(self, nums: list[int], target: int) -> list[int]:\n        seen: dict[int, int] = {}\n        for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n            partner = target - x\n            if partner in seen:  # viz:check\n                return [seen[partner], i]  # viz:found\n            seen[x] = i  # viz:store\n        return []\n",
    "explanation": "The loop visits each number once, and each dict lookup and insert takes O(1) time on average, so the time is O(n). When the pair is only found at the last number, every earlier number is in `seen` by then: up to n − 1 entries, so the space is O(n).",
    "toolkit": ["enumerate"]
  },
